## Decisions

- d1 `packages/coding-agent/test/bench-auth-fallback.test.ts` inline streamSimple mocks at ~line 346 (bench OAuth account selection) and ~line 655 (bench service tier): **rewrite**. The left-added tests build a streamSimple mock by wrapping a fakeStream() iterator with Object.assign(iterator, { result: stream.result }). Upstream's AssistantMessageEventStream type (as used by the streamSimple signature) is not satisfied by that plain object, so the mock no longer type-checks after the merge. Appending `as unknown as AssistantMessageEventStream` — exactly the convention already used by the fakeStream/emptyStream helpers in this same file (lines 56 and 71) — restores type compatibility without changing any runtime behaviour, so the left-added tests keep asserting what they were written to assert. No production file is touched.
- d2 `packages/coding-agent/test/tools/wait.test.ts` the 29m test's isolated settings object (line ~212, inside test.skipIf(\"a fixed 29-minute poll window holds every wait that long, without climbing\")): **rewrite**. The test builds its session as `{ ...session(manager), settings: Settings.isolated({ "async.pollWaitDuration": "29m" }) }`, which replaces the shared `session()` helper's settings wholesale and so drops the helper's `"launch.enabled": false`. `cfgLaunchEnabled` defaults to true, so `WaitTool.execute` takes its launch branch and awaits `listServicesTolerant` -> `daemonClientForProject` -> `SocketDaemonClient.#connectOnce`, whose retry loop is driven by `Date.now()` and `Bun.sleep` — both on the fake clock the test controls — and whose broker cannot start here (the worker exits with "Bun runtime must be >= 1.4.4 (found v1.4.2)"; `engines.bun` is >=1.4.4 in left, right and merged alike). The wait therefore never reaches its own `setTimeout(windowMs)`, so advancing the fake clock by the poll window cannot settle it and the test times out at 5s. Adding `"launch.enabled": false` restores the helper's launch isolation and keeps real daemon I/O out of a fake-timer test, without touching the fixed 29-minute window under test: both waits still assert "within 29m" and "up to 29m", and the file goes from 45 pass/1 fail to 46 pass/0 fail. Test-only; no product code changes, since every file in the chain (src/tools/wait.ts, src/launch/services.ts, src/launch/client.ts, src/subprocess/worker-client.ts, src/cli.ts, src/tools/settings.ts, src/config/settings.ts, src/async/job-control.ts, packages/utils/package.json) is identical between merged and left, so the failure is pre-existing on the fork rather than a merge regression.

